attribution: Traditional English tune and words, registered at the Stationers' Company in 1580 and printed in A Handful of Pleasant Delights (1584); public domain worldwide.
tags: english, renaissance, ballad, classic

# Written in 3/4 (one bar per half of the usual 6/8 bar), so the beat is the quarter
# note and the long notes are "(2)". The raised 6th/7th (C#, D#) are the tune's own.

chords:
| Em | G | D | Bm |
| Em | Em | B7 | B7 |
| Em | G | D | Bm |
| Em | B7 | Em | Em |
| G | G | D | Bm |
| Em | Em | B7 | B7 |
| G | G | D | Bm |
| Em | B7 | Em | Em |

melody:
# Verse 1
A-las, my love, you do me wrong,
E4 G4(2) A4 B4. ~C5/ B4 A4(2) F#4 D4. ~E4/
to cast me off dis-cour-teous-ly;
F#4 G4(2) E4 E4. ~D#4/ E4 F#4(2) D#4 B3(2)
and I have loved you oh so long,
E4 G4(2) A4 B4. ~C5/ B4 A4(2) F#4 D4. ~E4/
de-light-ing in your com-pa-ny.
F#4 G4. ~F#4/ E4 D#4. ~C#4/ D#4 E4(2) E4 E4(3)

# Chorus
Green-sleeves was all my joy,
D5(3) D5. ~C5/ B4 A4(2) F#4 D4. ~E4/ ~F#4
Green-sleeves was my de-light,
G4(2) ~E4 E4. ~D#4/ E4 F#4(2) D#4 B3(3)
Green-sleeves was my heart of gold,
D5(3) D5. C5/ B4 A4(2) F#4 D4. ~E4/
and who but my la-dy Green-sleeves.
F#4 G4. F#4/ E4 D#4. ~C#4/ D#4 E4(3) E4(3)
